{
  "skill": "# Temporal contract skill: ownership, completion, and effects\n\nPurpose: complete the original maintenance task while checking timing and ownership where they affect required behavior. Derive contracts from the task and project evidence.\n\nS1 Applicability. Check whether work can finish after its initiating access, operation, or consumer changes; callbacks can run during setup, cleanup, scheduling, or publication; or one result feeds effects with different validity rules. If none applies, briefly record why and continue the maintenance task.\n\nS2 Establish evidence. Read the entry point, producer or decoder, completion path, ownership transitions, cancellation path, and affected consumers. Inspect adjacent implementations for supported patterns, not automatic specifications. Record source locations for consequential facts. Separate explicit requirements, observed behavior, hypotheses, and unavailable evidence. A familiar pattern, function name, or passing self-written check does not establish a contract.\n\nS3 Define identities, effects, and states. Where the source distinguishes them, track the resource, access or visit, physical attempt, and accepted result. For each effect, including waiting indicators, history, current display, delivery, notification, and scheduled work, state which identity must be valid when it occurs. Identify what each status means from the producer and consumers before treating it as complete or pending. For a pending interval, determine the observable relationship among in-progress state, scheduled state, and the next check. Determine whether cancellation prevents new input, stops a scheduled callback, aborts running work, or releases only a consumer. Do not use one stale-result guard for every effect without evidence.\n\nS4 Trace the full handoff. Follow the order in which listeners are attached, work starts, handles become available, ownership is published, state changes, and cleanup runs. At calls into external or user code, consider synchronous reentry. Recheck ownership after such calls when later steps depend on the original owner. If cleanup can trigger another access, ensure the old attempt cannot overwrite the new owner or its effects. If startup can produce an immediate result, verify that its intended consumers are ready and that each effect follows its own contract.\n\nS5 Compare concrete event orders. Using project-supported APIs, exercise ordinary completion; pending followed by ready; completion after close, switch, or disposal; reuse of the same resource under a new access; replacement or retry within an access; and synchronous callback reentry where supported. When results can settle out of order, compare normal and reversed completion order. Derive ordering from supported identity or arrival sequence; do not invent a payload schema. Observe each relevant effect separately, including effects of superseded work that the contract retains. For scheduled work, inspect state before scheduling, while waiting, when a callback fires, and after completion; compare these with close, reopen, replacement, unmount, and a callback that runs during scheduling when the API permits it.\n\nS6 Preserve established behavior. Keep public interfaces, unrelated consumers of shared helpers, ordinary streaming, records, diagnostics, cleanup, and adjacent behavior consistent with the task. Distinguish an access ending from an attempt ending: the same resource may be reused, and a retry may keep the access while replacing its attempt. Do not infer that closing a view cancels a background obligation or that a stored effect must disappear with a visible one. Resolve these questions from source and task evidence; otherwise mark them unknown.\n\nS7 Repair and verify. Implement the smallest supported change that completes the maintenance task. Run relevant available checks and inspect their actual output. Add focused checks for meaningful untested event orders, including a boundary or reentry path when applicable. Assert state throughout transitions and each separate effect, including ownership and waiting state, rather than only the final result or that code ran. A local harness establishes only the paths it drives. A syntax check establishes syntax only; unavailable dependencies and failed commands establish no behavioral pass. If a check fails, revise the repair and rerun the relevant check. Finish with the maintenance result, what was verified, and explicit remaining uncertainty. Contract discussion alone is not completion.",
  "change_notes": [
    {
      "step": "Evidence",
      "change": "Require producer, scheduling, and consumer evidence before assigning status or cancellation meaning.",
      "feedback_basis": "The execution read the hook and adjacent component but did not read the preparation API source; observations alone did not prove contract understanding."
    },
    {
      "step": "Contract",
      "change": "Make waiting state a separate effect with checks across the pending interval.",
      "feedback_basis": "Grounded feedback reports a waiting-state mismatch during pending preparation; it supplies no new status schema."
    },
    {
      "step": "Validation",
      "change": "Check intermediate states and separate effects under concrete event orders; report the limits of each check.",
      "feedback_basis": "Only a syntax check visibly passed. Dependencies were missing, and the whole behavior score was 0.0; the precise cause remains unknown."
    }
  ]
}
